WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.838
A rotation by ninety degrees has

2
00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:08.592
eigenvalues plus and minus i

3
00:00:08.684 --> 00:00:11.438
so complex numbers are already

4
00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:12.815
in the course.

5
00:00:12.815 --> 00:00:15.293
Here is the complex plane

6
00:00:15.293 --> 00:00:17.864
one is a step to the right

7
00:00:17.864 --> 00:00:19.700
and i is a step up.

8
00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:23.737
Now take the vector with entries one and i.

9
00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:26.309
The usual length squared adds

10
00:00:26.395 --> 00:00:28.710
the squares of the entries.

11
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.425
One squared is one.

12
00:00:30.425 --> 00:00:31.711
And i squared

13
00:00:31.711 --> 00:00:34.797
multiplying by i is a quarter turn

14
00:00:34.797 --> 00:00:37.112
so i times i is minus one.

15
00:00:37.112 --> 00:00:38.570
The sum is zero.

16
00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.320
A vector that is clearly not zero

17
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.120
with length zero?

18
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.520
Pause and think: what went wrong?

19
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.269
The fix is to conjugate one side.

20
00:00:54.365 --> 00:00:57.247
The conjugate mirrors a number

21
00:00:57.343 --> 00:01:00.800
in the real axis: i becomes minus i.

22
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.546
Then i bar times i is minus i times i

23
00:01:04.642 --> 00:01:07.043
a quarter turn from minus

24
00:01:07.139 --> 00:01:09.060
i lands on plus one.

25
00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:13.586
So z H z, the conjugate transpose times z

26
00:01:13.586 --> 00:01:15.773
is one plus one: two.

27
00:01:15.773 --> 00:01:19.948
It adds the squared sizes of the entries

28
00:01:19.948 --> 00:01:22.533
and it is never negative.

29
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:26.808
For three plus four i and one minus two i

30
00:01:26.808 --> 00:01:29.890
twenty-five plus five, thirty.

31
00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.501
The inner product becomes

32
00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:35.395
x conjugate transpose, times y

33
00:01:35.486 --> 00:01:38.199
and orthogonal means that this

34
00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.279
inner product is zero.

35
00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:41.184
In NumPy

36
00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:44.530
v dot conjugates its first argument

37
00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.520
the at sign does not.

38
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.066
The complex version of a symmetric

39
00:01:50.153 --> 00:01:53.119
matrix is Hermitian: A H equals A.

40
00:01:53.119 --> 00:01:53.642
Here

41
00:01:53.642 --> 00:01:57.567
the entry above the diagonal is one minus i

42
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:01.144
and the entry below is its mirror image

43
00:02:01.144 --> 00:02:02.191
one plus i.

44
00:02:02.191 --> 00:02:05.418
The diagonal entries, two and three

45
00:02:05.418 --> 00:02:08.820
must be their own mirror images: real.

46
00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:13.332
Trace five, determinant six minus two: four.

47
00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:16.832
So the eigenvalues are one and four

48
00:02:16.832 --> 00:02:19.008
both on the real axis

49
00:02:19.008 --> 00:02:22.130
although the matrix is not real.

50
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:26.957
The eigenvectors are one minus i, minus one

51
00:02:26.957 --> 00:02:29.196
and one minus i, two.

52
00:02:29.196 --> 00:02:31.231
With the conjugate

53
00:02:31.231 --> 00:02:35.708
their inner product is two minus two: zero

54
00:02:35.708 --> 00:02:36.929
orthogonal.

55
00:02:36.929 --> 00:02:41.203
Without it you get minus two minus two i

56
00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:45.680
and would wrongly call them not orthogonal.

57
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:49.409
The complex version of an orthogonal

58
00:02:49.496 --> 00:02:52.470
matrix is unitary: U H U equals I.

59
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:54.045
It keeps lengths

60
00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.931
so every eigenvalue has size one

61
00:02:57.019 --> 00:02:59.468
and sits on the unit circle.

62
00:02:59.468 --> 00:03:02.529
The ninety degree rotation is one

63
00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:05.416
its eigenvalues plus i and minus

64
00:03:05.503 --> 00:03:07.340
i are on that circle.

65
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.442
The most important unitary matrix

66
00:03:11.442 --> 00:03:15.483
up to a factor, is the Fourier matrix.

67
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:19.109
Let w be e to the two pi i over n

68
00:03:19.212 --> 00:03:21.595
a point one n-th of the

69
00:03:21.699 --> 00:03:23.978
way around the circle.

70
00:03:23.978 --> 00:03:28.640
Entry j, k of F is w to the power j times k.

71
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.561
For n equals four, w is i.

72
00:03:31.561 --> 00:03:34.033
Column zero is all ones.

73
00:03:34.132 --> 00:03:37.494
Column one walks around the circle

74
00:03:37.593 --> 00:03:41.943
a quarter turn at a time: one, i, minus one

75
00:03:41.943 --> 00:03:42.833
minus i.

76
00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:46.591
Column two jumps back and forth: one

77
00:03:46.591 --> 00:03:49.261
minus one, one, minus one

78
00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.634
the fastest oscillation

79
00:03:51.733 --> 00:03:53.908
four samples can show.

80
00:03:53.908 --> 00:03:57.270
Column three walks the other way.

81
00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.915
The columns are orthogonal

82
00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:03.740
each of length two, the square root of four.

83
00:04:03.740 --> 00:04:05.950
So F H F is four times I

84
00:04:06.035 --> 00:04:08.330
and the inverse of F is its

85
00:04:08.415 --> 00:04:10.625
conjugate divided by four.

86
00:04:10.625 --> 00:04:12.580
No elimination needed.

87
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:13.892
Why care?

88
00:04:13.973 --> 00:04:16.493
Take four readings of something

89
00:04:16.574 --> 00:04:19.826
that repeats every four hours: two, one

90
00:04:19.826 --> 00:04:20.721
zero, one.

91
00:04:20.721 --> 00:04:23.973
Multiply by the inverse Fourier matrix

92
00:04:23.973 --> 00:04:27.387
the coefficients are one, one half, zero

93
00:04:27.387 --> 00:04:28.200
one half.

94
00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.113
That says: an average of one

95
00:04:31.113 --> 00:04:32.704
plus a slow wave

96
00:04:32.793 --> 00:04:35.179
a cosine that completes one

97
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:38.626
cycle in four hours, and nothing fast.

98
00:04:38.626 --> 00:04:41.896
Multiplying by F, or by its inverse

99
00:04:41.896 --> 00:04:44.990
is the discrete Fourier transform.

100
00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.659
Now check with NumPy.

101
00:04:47.758 --> 00:04:51.339
Surely its f f t function multiplies

102
00:04:51.438 --> 00:04:52.930
by this same F?

103
00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:54.559
Not quite.

104
00:04:54.559 --> 00:04:58.439
Take a single spike in position one.

105
00:04:58.439 --> 00:05:03.159
F times it is column one: one, i, minus one

106
00:05:03.159 --> 00:05:06.830
minus i, walking counterclockwise.

107
00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:10.932
NumPy returns one, minus i, minus one, i

108
00:05:10.932 --> 00:05:14.044
the conjugate, walking clockwise.

109
00:05:14.044 --> 00:05:17.705
It uses e to the minus two pi i over n

110
00:05:17.705 --> 00:05:21.366
and puts the one over n in its inverse.

111
00:05:21.366 --> 00:05:25.301
Both are correct for their own convention

112
00:05:25.393 --> 00:05:28.413
check the sign before you compare

113
00:05:28.504 --> 00:05:30.060
a book with code.

114
00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:31.574
To sum up.

115
00:05:31.574 --> 00:05:34.937
Complex vectors need the conjugate

116
00:05:34.937 --> 00:05:37.365
z H z adds squared sizes.

117
00:05:37.365 --> 00:05:41.288
Hermitian matrices have real eigenvalues

118
00:05:41.288 --> 00:05:44.184
and unitary ones keep lengths

119
00:05:44.184 --> 00:05:47.640
with eigenvalues on the unit circle.

120
00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.996
And the Fourier matrix walks

121
00:05:50.068 --> 00:05:51.429
around that circle

122
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.936
with orthogonal columns that split

123
00:05:54.008 --> 00:05:55.870
a signal into frequencies.

124
00:05:56.450 --> 00:05:57.609
In part two

125
00:05:57.609 --> 00:06:01.471
multiplying by F costs n squared steps.

126
00:06:01.568 --> 00:06:04.079
The fast Fourier transform

127
00:06:04.175 --> 00:06:06.010
does it in n log n.
